{
  "analysis": "The review describes Poké Balls being consumed unusually quickly after purchase, allegedly because captures break out more often, so the player must buy more. This supports Pay to Progress because payment is presented as necessary to continue capture gameplay, and Illusion of Control because the reviewer alleges purchase-linked manipulation of capture outcomes. The edit states that the review was not written by the reviewer, which fits store-review impersonation. Intermediate Currency is rejected because Poké Balls are items, not currency; Waste Aversion and Reward Mania are not supported because no stranded currency or reward-delivery mechanic is described. Reciprocity is rejected because the reviewer denies actually talking to other players.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rationale": "The span describes needing to spend money on more Poké Balls to continue capture gameplay."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that purchasing is associated with manipulated or worse capture outcomes."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW",
      "rationale": "The edit describes a store review appearing under the player's identity without being authored by them, fitting the store-review impersonation boundary."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes owned robots becoming weaker after game changes, which satisfies Power Creep. It also describes spending thousands and maxing options as necessary to win, satisfying Pay to Progress through a pay-to-win route. Fighting overpowered real players describes Competition, while the algorithm assigning mismatched opponents describes hidden matchmaking manipulation under Illusion of Control. No search is needed; other labels are not supported by a specific mechanic span.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "after each change, my robots get weaker and weaker",
      "rationale": "The span describes owned game items losing effectiveness after changes."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it has become a scam unless you're willing to dump thousands into it and max out every option",
      "rationale": "The span describes spending money and maximizing paid options as necessary to win."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the algorithm will put you against real players that are way over powered for you to fight",
      "rationale": "The span describes the player competing in fights against other real players."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the algorithm will put you against real players that are way over powered for you to fight",
      "rationale": "The span attributes unfair outcomes to hidden algorithmic matchmaking."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes spending money as a route through game progression in \"buy their way through the game,\" which satisfies M_PayToProgress. The mention of hacking describes player conduct rather than a game design mechanic, so it is rejected under R2. \"Nobody enjoys grinding a game anymore\" is a general evaluation without a named repetitive task tied to progress, so T_Grinding is rejected under its boundary rule. Having an inventory full of useless gear does not describe stranded currency, capacity pressure, or forfeited value, so M_WasteAversion is rejected.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players who either buy their way through the game",
      "rationale": "This describes players spending money to advance through the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes spending real money for stronger or advantageous robots, supporting M_PayToProgress. It also describes an owned robot being nerfed after a month while newer robots are introduced, supporting M_PowerCreep. The reference to clans of paying players defeating the reviewer describes player-versus-player competition, supporting S_Competition. Lag is attributed to a technical problem, so it is not coded under R11. No scarcity, gambling, collection, or appointment mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "this is one of the worst pay to win games out there",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes spending as a route to in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "one month later, the robot you have get nerfed and another new robot introduced",
      "rationale": "An owned robot loses value through nerfing while newer robots are introduced, indicating purchased-item devaluation over time."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "clans made out of players who have spent a lot of money will get you",
      "rationale": "The span describes the player being opposed and defeated by other player clans in the multiplayer game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes accumulated bag contents and coins, including purchased coins, being lost after leaving and returning. This supports P_EasyToGetHardToLose because exit causes forfeiture of held value, and T_MandatoryMarathon because stopping the session results in loss. M_IntermediateCurrency also applies because the review explicitly describes coins as purchased. M_WasteAversion and M_PayToProgress are rejected: no stranded currency shortfall or payment-for-progress gate is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "I leave the game with my bag full only to come back with it empty and coins that I earned and purchased used",
      "rationale": "Leaving and returning causes accumulated inventory and coins to be forfeited, making disengagement costly."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_MandatoryMarathon",
      "span": "I leave the game with my bag full only to come back with it empty and coins that I earned and purchased used",
      "rationale": "The session cannot be safely ended because leaving results in the loss of held value."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "coins that I earned and purchased",
      "rationale": "The review describes in-game coins as a currency acquired through purchase."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes prolonged progress-oriented grinding, purchases of Gold/Event Passes, and an update that devalues previously earned hero-equipment progress by giving equivalent progress to new players. I considered T_PlayingByAppointment and T_DailyRewards because of \"playing actively every day,\" but no reward reset, expiry, or game-imposed schedule is described. M_PayToProgress and M_IntermediateCurrency were rejected because the review does not state that payment is required for progress or that real money is converted into currency. S_Competition was rejected because \"overtake me\" does not identify a competitive mode. T_InfiniteTreadmill and P_EasyToGetHardToLose were rejected because years of remaining progress does not establish endless completion or reluctance to quit. The search resolved Gold Pass and Event Pass as purchasable pass features, supporting M_RecurringFee. ([support.supercell.com](https://support.supercell.com/clash-of-clans/en/articles/events-2.html?utm_source=openai))",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "After a year of grinding",
      "rationale": "Explicitly describes prolonged grinding undertaken in the context of game progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "buying gold/event passes",
      "rationale": "Describes purchasing pass-based monetization, an indicator of recurring-fee design."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "an update that invalidates most of the progress I achieved. New players get my current level of hero equipment progress for free, and can easily overtake me, while I get nothing and am still years away from maxing it out, despite playing actively every day and spending money on the game",
      "rationale": "The update makes previously earned and paid-for progress substantially less valuable by granting equivalent progress to newer players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Clash of Clans Gold Pass event pass",
  "search_result": "Official Supercell support identifies Gold Pass and Event Pass as purchasable Clash of Clans pass features; this resolved the review's pass reference. ([support.supercell.com](https://support.supercell.com/clash-of-clans/en/articles/events-2.html?utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes purchasing in-game coins with real money: \"$20 for only 100k or so coins.\" This satisfies M_IntermediateCurrency because real money is exchanged for virtual currency. The poor prize value does not itself describe a codable mechanic under R2. M_Gambling is rejected because the review does not clearly describe paying to participate in a chance-based reward mechanic; M_PayToProgress is rejected because the purchase is not explicitly tied to progressing; M_WasteAversion is rejected because no stranded currency, capacity limit, or specific shortfall is described; and P_RewardMania is rejected because the complaint concerns prize value rather than reward delivery.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "$20 for only 100k or so coins",
      "rationale": "The span describes exchanging real-world money for in-game coins."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes requesting a resource from an existing clan, which satisfies Reciprocity. It also describes misleading wording in an in-game coin promotion, satisfying Aesthetic Manipulation. I rejected UI Misdirection and Easy to Purchase because the clicking behavior is too unclear and does not explicitly describe a purchase interface or obstructed opt-out. Gambling and Reward Mania are not assigned because no real-money stake or sufficiently explicit random-reward mechanic is described. No temporal, progression, collection, or technical mechanic is stated.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "ask our clan for a stamp",
      "rationale": "The player describes asking existing clan members for an in-game resource."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_AestheticManipulation",
      "span": "The wording makes you think you are going to get more than you think.",
      "rationale": "Misleading promotional wording manipulates the player's understanding of the reward offered."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive 5v5 matchmaking through the references to \"match making\" and \"teammates\", so S_Competition applies. It also alleges that matchmaking deliberately assigns worse teammates based on whether the player performs well or poorly, which is a claim that outcomes are tuned by a hidden rule and triggers P_IllusionOfControl. Anger, frustration, wasting time and energy, wanting money, and copying another game are evaluations or general complaints without a codable mechanic; no grinding or payment mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "The match making, completely illogical",
      "rationale": "Matchmaking in the named 5v5 game organizes players into competitive matches against other players."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "if you play good,game will give you bad teammates expecting you to carry and if you play bad,game will give you more bad teammates saying that it's so called \"skill based matching\"",
      "rationale": "The span alleges that matchmaking outcomes are deliberately tuned according to the player's performance, a hidden manipulation of match determination."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a gacha mechanic and says players are encouraged to purchase into it because they like the characters. Gacha supports P_RewardMania, and \"buy into the gatcha\" describes a purchased chance-based stake, supporting M_Gambling. The character appeal is presented as the design's persuasion mechanism, supporting P_AestheticManipulation. M_PowerCreep is rejected because the review explicitly says the game does not use it; duration of play is not a mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "buy into the gatcha",
      "rationale": "This describes purchasing access to a gacha mechanic involving chance-based rewards."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_AestheticManipulation",
      "span": "They simply make you like the character.",
      "rationale": "The game uses emotional attachment to characters to influence players toward the gacha."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the gatcha",
      "rationale": "The review names a gacha mechanic, an unpredictable reward-delivery structure."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes duplicate sticker drops and an inability to complete all sets without payment. P_RewardMania applies because duplicate sticker delivery is an unwanted outcome of the sticker-pack system, and P_CompleteTheCollection applies because finishing all sets is the stated goal. M_PayToProgress applies because payment is described as necessary to finish them. M_Gambling was rejected under its boundary rule because the review does not state that money is spent on a chance-based stake. M_DeceptiveLuxury was rejected because no scarcity, rarity, or limited-time purchase condition is described; no social, daily-return, technical, or time-gating mechanic is present.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "never lets you finish all the sets unless you pay",
      "rationale": "Payment is described as necessary to complete the game's sets, making progress contingent on spending."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "finish all the sets",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the goal of completing an in-game collection."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "always get duplicates.",
      "rationale": "The span describes repeated unwanted duplicate sticker outcomes in the sticker collection system."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "MONOPOLY GO stickers duplicate sticker packs complete sets mechanic",
  "search_result": "Confirmed that MONOPOLY GO uses collectible sticker sets and that sticker packs can produce duplicate stickers, resolving the sticker mechanic. ([play.google.com](https://play.google.com/store/apps/editorial?hl=en&id=mc_games_editorialevergreen_monopoly_go_all_about_stickers_postinstall_fcp&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an initial file download followed by another in-game download, indicating fragmented downloads that obscure or split the game's actual download requirements. The matchmaking problem is attributed to a Wi-Fi/technical issue, so it does not support a design label under R11. I rejected S_Competition because matchmaking is mentioned only as malfunctioning, not as competition between players, and rejected T_PlayingByAppointment because no imposed waiting timer or schedule is described.",
