{
  "analysis": "The review describes in-app purchase offers appearing before play, which supports Easy to Purchase because the offers intercept gameplay. It also describes progress requiring money and a shifting meta that makes the newest bot necessary, supporting Pay to Progress and Power Creep. I rejected Advertisement because the purchase offers are not described as ads, FOMO because no explicit pressure to continue or fear of missing out is stated, and ad-related labeling because the final malformed phrase does not clearly describe forced or rewarded ad viewing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "everytime you log in, your screen has spam of so called \"good deals\" in which I mean in app purchases before you can actually play your game",
      "rationale": "Purchase offers are presented before the player can access gameplay, so the solicitation intercepts play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you want to progress without paying money, goodluck! Even if you wanted to do f2p, you would not be able to keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot with money instead of earning it",
      "rationale": "The span states that progressing and keeping up requires buying the newest bot with real money."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "you would not be able to keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot with money instead of earning it",
      "rationale": "A shifting meta requiring the newest bot indicates that newer releases diminish the competitive value of earlier holdings and drive new purchases."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a pay-to-win monetization structure, long base upgrade timers, endlessly scaling progression, UI confusion intended to encourage purchases, and player conflict involving attacks on unaffiliated players. I considered T_Grinding but rejected it because \"more grind\" names no repetitive activity, and S_ForcedFellowship because being pressured into an alliance through attacks fits coerced grouping without described obligation to group members. M_EasyToPurchase is also not assigned because no specific purchase prompt, default, or reduced-friction interface is described. The endlessly scaling goals support T_InfiniteTreadmill because the game presents no meaningful attainable endpoint.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "pay to win way",
      "rationale": "The span describes spending money to gain an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rationale": "Base upgrade timers impose waits controlled by the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rationale": "Endless scaling and the absence of a meaningful endpoint describe a continually receding progression goal."
